<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasions of Anatolia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> were the campaigns by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> subdued the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> in the mid-13th century. A Mongol army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> at Köse Dağ in 1243, making the sultanate a tributary, and Baiju defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> again in 1256. Anatolia then came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, whose decline in the early 14th century allowed Turkish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, to rise.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasions-of-anatolia.jpg" alt="Mongol invasions of Anatolia" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_invasions_of_Anatolia_1231-1232,_1242-1243.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
